On Tue, Feb 06, 2018 at 10:12:28PM -0800, Linus Torvalds wrote:
> On Tue, Feb 6, 2018 at 9:37 PM, Takashi Iwai <tiwai@suse.de> wrote:

> >> Because some of those merges look very Lovecraftian, with the whole
> >> gigantic octopus merge theme. Cthulhu and tentacles galore.

Right, it's basically a couple of octopus merges split up into chunks of
5 branches because you didn't like the octopus merges and also said you
don't like ongoing development branches with no or few non-merge
commits.  I'm keeping everything on topic branches (mainly per device)
because we get problems with people discovering that they need branches
in other trees and the entire tree gets too big to merge elsewhere.
It's also occasionally it's useful to be able to just throw away a
branch if it turns out to have been a bad idea.

> For example, these merges bring in basically *nothing* new (ok, so
> some of them bring in a couple of commits, but see the pattern here?):

...

> It's the *MERGES* that need to be stopped. I don't know what the hell
> Mark is doing. There's some kind of completely mindless and crazy
> merging going on.

> And I think it's entirely automated *GARBAGE*. Look at this:

Indeed, it's 100% automated.  The script just goes through and merges
all the branches it sees and relies on git to elide empty merges which
happen, having first merged in any older tags to avoid duplication of in
flight merges.

I'm frankly a little bit confused about what went wrong here, usually
git does manage to elide empty merges - for example, if I ask git to
merge four branches two of which are already merged:

   $ git merge asoc/fix/compress asoc/fix/core asoc/fix/mtk asoc/fix/stm

it gives me "Merge: 7614447873f9 06190937b9de 748bce8e6c7e" (ie, two of
four branches merged) which looks a lot neater.  This time something
caused git to miss some of the merges, I expect it's the amount that
happened since my original tag due to the combination of that being a
bit early for Takashi's vacation and then the merge window being delayed
by several weeks meaning that things were too far back in history.  I
can't actually see anything in the manual page on this behaviour.

Anyway, here's a redone pull request.  The diffstat still has far too
much stuff in it for reasons I can't quite figure out, AFAICT it's that
some of the fix branches are based on earlier pull requests but I don't
want to get myself in a mess trying to rebase to confirm.  There's 8
merges here, two are from the development history (the twl fix was a
single patch to two branches and some of the compress development needed
the corresponding fixes branch).  The remaining 6 are an attempt to
balance not having large octopus merges with not having huge numbers of
single merge commits.  Hopefully this is OK for you, if not let me know
what you're looking for.

The following changes since commit 031734b7d6532633d0cde73475c30646bf37cd6d:

  ASoC: soc-core: add missing EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL() for snd_soc_rtdcom_lookup (2018-01-18 11:44:54 +0000)

are available in the Git repository at:

  https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/broonie/sound.git tags/asoc-v4.16-5

for you to fetch changes up to 3d0a352a552e6cdf052e8baf66f1fe6418c122fe:

  Merge remote-tracking branches 'asoc/topic/sam9x5_wm8731', 'asoc/topic/sgtl5000' and 'asoc/topic/sun8i-codec' into asoc-next (2018-02-07 11:25:47 +0000)

----------------------------------------------------------------
ASoC: Updates for v4.16

With the merge window having been delayed for another week here's
another batch of updates that came in during that week.  There's a few
important fixes in here, mainly a fix for I/O on a number of devices
caused by some of the component rework and a fix for a potential issue
if more than one component in a link provides compressed operations.
The I/O fixes are particularly important as the problem causes a power
regression on a number of OMAP platforms.

